I have enough context to select the appropriate specialist archetypes. The diff introduces a large Python foundation tree (`python/`) — 11 runtime modules, 11 test files, CI jobs, Makefile targets, linter configs, and allowlist updates. This is a greenfield Python codebase with security-critical modules (`redact.py`) and subprocess/external-tool integrations (`git.py`, `gh.py`, `agents.py`).

{"archetypes":[{"name":"python-stdlib-boundary","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"New python/ tree has a hard stdlib-only rule for runtime modules; any stray third-party import at any AST depth would break the live path when cutover happens.","prompt_body":"Audit every runtime Python module in the diff (config.py, proc.py, errors.py, outcomes.py, run_context.py, logging_util.py, redact.py, retry.py, git.py, gh.py, agents.py) for imports that fall outside sys.stdlib_module_names or the sibling python/ tree. Pay special attention to function-local and class-body imports (lazy imports) since test_stdlib_only.py claims to walk all AST nodes — verify the enforcement test itself is correct and covers conditional branches. Also check that test files (test_*.py) are not accidentally importable as runtime modules by the stdlib-only test. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"redact-parity","focus_area":"security","weight":2,"rationale":"redact.py is security-critical and must produce byte-identical output to the bash redact-secrets.sh/redact-tmpdir-paths.sh; any divergence means secret leakage on the future Python path.","prompt_body":"Examine python/redact.py for correctness of each secret-family regex: sk-/sk-ant- prefixes, GitHub token prefixes (ghp_/gho_/ghu_/ghs_/ghr_/github_pat_), AWS AKIA pattern, JWT eyJ pattern, and multi-line PEM private-key blocks. Specifically verify the PEM fail-closed path: a BEGIN private-key marker with no matching END before EOF must drop the entire tail and emit a visible truncation marker without leaking any key material. Check that the idempotence claim holds (re-running redact on already-redacted text is a no-op). Look at test_redact.py to confirm it covers all families including the unterminated-PEM edge case and that the bash-parity test actually invokes both implementations and compares their stdout byte-for-byte. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"gh-retry-idempotency","focus_area":"code-quality","weight":1,"rationale":"gh.py distinguishes retry-safe read ops from non-idempotent mutating ops; a misclassification could create duplicate PRs, comments, or issue edits in production.","prompt_body":"Review python/gh.py's per-operation retry policy: confirm that pr_view, pr_for_branch, run_list, run_view, and failed_jobs are wrapped with retry while pr_create, pr_merge, run_rerun, issue_comment, and issue_edit are not. Check that pr_create's deduplication path (look up existing PR by branch before any retry attempt) is correctly implemented — specifically that it cannot race itself or return a stale hit as a false positive. Verify that retry exhaustion returns the last result rather than raising, and that a transient error on a mutating op surfaces clearly rather than silently swallowing the failure. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"ci-job-wiring","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"Two new CI jobs (python-lint, python-tests) are added and ci-failed-jobs.sh must recognize them; a mismatch causes ship-pr to classify them as ci-local-unfixable and break the strangler boundary.","prompt_body":"Check that the two new CI job names added to scripts/ci-failed-jobs.sh exactly match the job id fields in .github/workflows/ci.yaml (python-lint, python-tests). Verify the python-lint job includes the setup-node step (required for pyright's Node runtime) and that python-tests intentionally omits it. Confirm that both jobs use the correct cache-dependency-path (requirements-dev.txt for lint, requirements-test.txt for tests) and that the Makefile py-lint / py-test recipes use cd python && ... rather than working-directory, which would behave differently if run from a subdirectory. Check that neither job is wired into the make lint umbrella target (per the plan: they stay explicit). Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"agents-waterfall-correctness","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The agents.py waterfall short-circuit logic is a port of ship-pr.sh's run_ci_fix_vendor; mismatched short-circuit conditions would cause wrong-tier selection or infinite fall-through in future phases.","prompt_body":"Examine python/agents.py's run_waterfall function and classify_launch_failure. Verify the short-circuit condition: the waterfall must stop on first-fixer non-health failures (other/parse/refusal/timeout/unknown) but fall through on health/auth/binary-missing failures. Check that the canonical failure token set (none/health/other/auth/binary-missing/health-probe/timeout/parse/refusal/unknown) is complete and matches the bash source in scripts/lib-external-launcher-common.sh. Confirm an empty or exhausted tier order yields a typed WaterfallResult with winning_tier=None rather than raising. Look at test_agents.py to confirm stub-runner tests cover both short-circuit and fall-through paths. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"gitleaks-allowlist-scope","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"The new .gitleaks.toml allowlist entries for python/test_redact.py and python/ cache directories create scanner blind spots; overly broad patterns could suppress real secret detections in the python/ subtree.","prompt_body":"Review the new .gitleaks.toml path allowlist entries: ^python/test_redact\\.py$, ^python/\\.venv/, ^python/\\.pytest_cache/, ^python/__pycache__/. Assess whether these regex anchors are tight enough — in particular, confirm that the .venv, .pytest_cache, and __pycache__ patterns cannot match paths outside the python/ subtree (e.g., a top-level __pycache__/ directory). Verify that the allowlist description in .gitleaks.toml accurately reflects the new entries and that the SECURITY.md note about TruffleHog being the live-credential backstop is sufficient given gitleaks will not scan test_redact.py. Check that python/ runtime modules (config.py, redact.py, etc.) are NOT in the allowlist and remain subject to full gitleaks scanning. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
